1. Why detect hidden PowerShell?
PowerShell supports launching processes with a hidden window.

For example:
powershell.exe -WindowStyle Hidden -Command "Get-Process"


An attacker may use this technique to reduce the visibility of malicious activity to an interactive user.

However, legitimate background automation can also use hidden execution.
2. Detection query
Create detections/hidden-powershell.kql:

//
// Detection: Hidden PowerShell Execution
// Platform: Microsoft Sentinel
// Data source: DeviceProcessEvents
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1059.001
//

DeviceProcessEvents
| where Timestamp >= ago(24h)
| where FileName in~ ("powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe")
| extend CommandLine = tostring(ProcessCommandLine)
| where CommandLine matches regex
    @"(?i)-(?:windowstyle|w)\s+['""]?hidden(?:['""]|\s|$)"
| project
    Timestamp,
    DeviceName,
    AccountName,
    FileName,
    ProcessCommandLine,
    InitiatingProcessFileName,
    InitiatingProcessCommandLine,
    ProcessId
| order by Timestamp desc



3. Understand the regex
(?i)-(?:windowstyle|w)\s+['""]?hidden(?:['""]|\s|$)


| Pattern | Meaning |
|---|---|
| (?i) | Case-insensitive matching |
| - | Parameter prefix |
| (?:windowstyle\|w) | Full or abbreviated parameter |
| \s+ | One or more spaces |
| ['""]? | Optional quote |
| hidden | Hidden window value |
| (?:['""]|\s|$) | Value boundary |
